# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
DAYS_3 = [
{"day":59,"book":"Galatians","ref":"Galatians 1-3","title":"No Other Gospel",
 "chapters":[
   ("Gal 1","Paul, astonished the Galatians are abandoning grace for a different 'gospel,' defends his apostleship as coming directly from Christ, not human appointment."),
   ("Gal 2","Paul recounts publicly confronting Peter for hypocritically withdrawing from Gentile believers, insisting that 'a person is justified by faith in Jesus Christ,' not by observing the law."),
   ("Gal 3","Paul argues from Abraham's example that faith, not law-keeping, has always been the basis of righteousness, and that the law's purpose was to lead people to Christ, not to save them."),
 ],
 "themes":["There is only one true gospel; even sincere additions to grace distort it.","The law was a guide pointing toward Christ, never a means of earning righteousness."],
 "expositor":("bruce","F. F. Bruce noted that Paul's public rebuke of Peter in Galatians 2 shows that even apostolic authority yields to gospel truth -- no leader's example or status can be allowed to compromise justification by faith alone."),
 "reflect":"Is there a subtle 'addition' to grace -- some extra requirement -- you've quietly attached to what makes you acceptable to God?"},

{"day":60,"book":"Galatians","ref":"Galatians 4-6","title":"Freedom, Sonship, and the Spirit's Fruit",
 "chapters":[
   ("Gal 4","Paul describes believers as adopted sons and daughters, no longer slaves under the law, using the allegory of Hagar and Sarah to contrast slavery and freedom."),
   ("Gal 5","Paul warns against using Christian liberty as an excuse for sinful indulgence, contrasting the 'works of the flesh' with the beautiful, Spirit-produced 'fruit of the Spirit.'"),
   ("Gal 6","Paul instructs believers to gently restore those caught in sin, to bear one another's burdens, and closes with the reminder that 'a man reaps what he sows.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["Christian freedom is freedom for love and Spirit-led living, not license for selfishness.","Spiritual fruit grows from the Spirit's work, not sheer willpower alone."],
 "expositor":("stott","Stott observed that Galatians 5's fruit of the Spirit is presented as a single, unified fruit (not 'fruits'), growing together as one organic outcome of walking by the Spirit, rather than isolated virtues to master individually by effort."),
 "reflect":"Which single quality of the fruit of the Spirit (love, joy, peace, patience, and so on) do you most sense the Spirit inviting you to grow in right now?"},

{"day":61,"book":"Ephesians","ref":"Ephesians 1-3","title":"Chosen, Redeemed, United",
 "chapters":[
   ("Eph 1","Paul praises God for the believer's staggering spiritual blessings -- chosen before creation, redeemed through Christ's blood, sealed with the Holy Spirit -- and prays for their eyes to be opened to this hope."),
   ("Eph 2","Paul describes salvation as entirely by grace through faith, 'not by works,' and celebrates that Christ has torn down the dividing wall of hostility between Jew and Gentile, making 'one new humanity.'"),
   ("Eph 3","Paul describes the 'mystery' now revealed -- Gentiles are fully co-heirs with Jewish believers -- and prays that believers would grasp the vast dimensions of Christ's love."),
 ],
 "themes":["Salvation is entirely a gift of grace, leaving no room for human boasting.","In Christ, previously hostile groups become one unified family."],
 "expositor":("wright","N. T. Wright has emphasized that Ephesians 2's 'dividing wall' language directly evokes the literal temple barrier separating Jews and Gentiles in Jerusalem, meaning Paul is describing not just individual salvation but a genuinely new, reconciled community."),
 "reflect":"Ephesians 2:8-9 grounds salvation entirely in grace. Do you ever slip into relating to God as if your standing depended on your performance rather than His gift?"},

{"day":62,"book":"Ephesians","ref":"Ephesians 4-6","title":"One Body, New Life, Full Armor",
 "chapters":[
   ("Eph 4","Paul urges unity worthy of the believers' calling, describes varied gifts given to equip the church, and calls for putting off the 'old self' for a renewed, Christlike way of life."),
   ("Eph 5","Paul instructs believers to imitate God's love, avoid the world's darkness, be filled with the Spirit, and describes mutual submission and sacrificial love within marriage."),
   ("Eph 6","Paul addresses family and workplace relationships, then closes with the famous image of the 'full armor of God' for standing firm against spiritual opposition."),
 ],
 "themes":["Church unity and holy living flow directly from believers' identity in Christ.","Spiritual warfare requires deliberately putting on God's provided armor, not relying on human strength."],
 "expositor":("carson","Carson has pointed out that Ephesians 6's armor imagery, written from a Roman prison, would have been vividly illustrated by the very soldier guarding Paul -- turning an everyday, familiar sight into a memorable picture of spiritual readiness."),
 "reflect":"Which piece of the armor of God (truth, righteousness, readiness, faith, salvation, the Spirit's word, prayer) feels least 'put on' in your life currently?"},

{"day":63,"book":"Philippians","ref":"Philippians 1-4","title":"Joy That Doesn't Depend on Circumstances",
 "chapters":[
   ("Phil 1","Paul rejoices even from prison that his imprisonment has actually advanced the gospel, and declares that 'to live is Christ and to die is gain.'"),
   ("Phil 2","Paul presents the great Christ-hymn of humble self-emptying and subsequent exaltation as the pattern believers are called to imitate in their own relationships."),
   ("Phil 3","Paul counts his impressive religious credentials as 'garbage' compared to knowing Christ, and presses on toward the goal like a runner, not claiming to have already arrived."),
   ("Phil 4","Paul urges the Philippians to rejoice always, present anxiety to God in prayer, and shares the secret of contentment in any circumstance, 'through him who gives me strength.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["Genuine joy is rooted in Christ, not in favorable circumstances.","Contentment is a learned discipline, grounded in Christ's strength, not natural temperament."],
 "expositor":("lewis","Lewis's own reflections on joy distinguished it sharply from mere pleasure or happiness dependent on circumstances -- an insight that fits Paul's ability to write a letter overflowing with joy from within a Roman prison cell."),
 "reflect":"Philippians 4:11-13 describes contentment as learned, not automatic. What circumstance are you currently needing to learn contentment within, rather than waiting for it to change first?"},

{"day":64,"book":"Colossians","ref":"Colossians 1-4","title":"The Supremacy and Sufficiency of Christ",
 "chapters":[
   ("Col 1","Paul exalts Christ as supreme over all creation -- 'in him all things were created' and 'in him all things hold together' -- the very fullness of God in bodily form."),
   ("Col 2","Paul warns against being taken captive by hollow philosophy, legalistic rules, or mystical experiences, insisting believers are already spiritually complete in Christ alone."),
   ("Col 3","Paul calls believers to set their minds on things above, put off old sinful patterns, and put on Christlike virtues, letting 'the peace of Christ rule' in their hearts."),
   ("Col 4","Paul closes with instructions on prayer, wise conduct toward outsiders, and warm personal greetings to co-workers in ministry."),
 ],
 "themes":["Christ alone is fully sufficient; no additional philosophy or ritual can improve on Him.","Genuine spiritual growth reshapes daily thought patterns and relationships, not just private belief."],
 "expositor":("guzik","Guzik notes that Colossians 2's warning against being 'taken captive' by empty philosophy applies just as directly to modern substitutes for Christ -- therapeutic self-help, political ideology, or mysticism -- as it did to the Colossian church's original mixture of influences."),
 "reflect":"Is there a modern 'philosophy' or system you've quietly let compete with, rather than submit to, the sufficiency of Christ?"},

{"day":65,"book":"1 Thessalonians","ref":"1 Thessalonians 1-3","title":"A Model Church, A Father's Affection",
 "chapters":[
   ("1 Thess 1","Paul commends the Thessalonians' faith, love, and hope, noting how their transformed lives from idolatry to serving the true God have become known 'everywhere.'"),
   ("1 Thess 2","Paul recalls his sincere, sacrificial ministry among them, comparing his care to that of a nursing mother and an encouraging father."),
   ("1 Thess 3","Paul expresses deep relief and joy at hearing of their continued faith despite persecution, praying earnestly for their continued growth in love and holiness."),
 ],
 "themes":["Genuine faith visibly transforms daily life, not just private belief.","Pastoral ministry, at its best, combines sacrificial love with earnest, ongoing concern."],
 "expositor":("wiersbe","Wiersbe highlighted Paul's tender parental imagery in 1 Thessalonians 2 as a model for all Christian ministry -- combining a mother's gentle nurture with a father's earnest encouragement, rather than distant, purely functional leadership."),
 "reflect":"Is there someone in your life who needs both gentle nurture and earnest encouragement from you right now, the way Paul describes caring for the Thessalonians?"},

{"day":66,"book":"1 Thessalonians","ref":"1 Thessalonians 4-5","title":"Holy Living While Waiting",
 "chapters":[
   ("1 Thess 4","Paul calls believers to sexual purity and honest work, then comforts those grieving believers who have died with the promise of a future resurrection and reunion at Christ's return."),
   ("1 Thess 5","Paul describes Christ's return as coming unexpectedly 'like a thief in the night,' urging believers to stay spiritually alert, and closes with rapid-fire practical exhortations, including 'rejoice always' and 'pray continually.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["The hope of resurrection genuinely comforts grief over believers who have died.","Spiritual alertness and consistent practical holiness should mark those awaiting Christ's return."],
 "expositor":("macarthur","MacArthur has taught that 1 Thessalonians 4's comfort about believers who have died is meant to reshape how Christians grieve -- not without real sorrow, but 'not... like the rest of mankind, who have no hope,' since resurrection, not merely memory, awaits them."),
 "reflect":"How does the hope of resurrection actually change (not just soften) the way you process grief over loss?"},

{"day":67,"book":"2 Thessalonians","ref":"2 Thessalonians 1-3","title":"Standing Firm Amid Confusion and Persecution",
 "chapters":[
   ("2 Thess 1","Paul encourages believers enduring persecution, assuring them that God's justice will ultimately be set right and that their endurance is evidence of genuine faith."),
   ("2 Thess 2","Paul corrects a false rumor that the 'day of the Lord' had already occurred, describing certain events (including 'the man of lawlessness') that must precede Christ's return."),
   ("2 Thess 3","Paul rebukes believers who had stopped working while idly waiting for Christ's return, insisting that 'the one who is unwilling to work shall not eat,' and calls for continued diligence."),
 ],
 "themes":["Present suffering does not contradict God's ultimate justice; it precedes it.","Anticipating Christ's return should produce diligence, not idle passivity."],
 "expositor":("henry","Matthew Henry noted that Paul's rebuke of idleness in 2 Thessalonians 3 shows that genuine end-times expectation should never become an excuse to abandon ordinary, responsible daily duties -- true hope makes people more faithful in the present, not less."),
 "reflect":"Is there a responsibility you've been neglecting under the guise of 'waiting on God' rather than diligently working while you wait?"},

{"day":68,"book":"1 Timothy","ref":"1 Timothy 1-3","title":"Guarding Sound Doctrine and Church Order",
 "chapters":[
   ("1 Tim 1","Paul charges Timothy to confront false teachers in Ephesus, recounting his own transformation from 'the worst of sinners' to a trophy of God's patient mercy."),
   ("1 Tim 2","Paul urges prayer for all people, including rulers, and addresses appropriate conduct and roles for men and women in public worship gatherings."),
   ("1 Tim 3","Paul lists the moral and relational qualifications required for church overseers (elders) and deacons, emphasizing proven character over mere talent or ambition."),
 ],
 "themes":["God's patient mercy toward even 'the worst of sinners' remains a pattern for others to find hope in.","Church leadership qualifications center on tested character, not simply skill or charisma."],
 "expositor":("piper","Piper has often pointed to Paul's testimony in 1 Timothy 1 -- 'the worst of sinners,' yet shown mercy 'as an example' -- as proof that no one's past disqualifies them from being transformed into a display case of God's patience for others watching."),
 "reflect":"Is there a part of your past you assume is too shameful to ever become, like Paul's, a testimony of God's patience to someone else?"},

{"day":69,"book":"1 Timothy","ref":"1 Timothy 4-6","title":"Godliness, Ministry, and Contentment",
 "chapters":[
   ("1 Tim 4","Paul warns of coming false asceticism, urging Timothy to train himself in godliness, which 'holds promise for both the present life and the life to come.'"),
   ("1 Tim 5","Paul gives practical guidance for caring for widows, honoring faithful elders, and handling accusations fairly within the church community."),
   ("1 Tim 6","Paul warns against the love of money as 'a root of all kinds of evil,' urging contentment with godliness, and charges Timothy to 'fight the good fight of the faith.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["Genuine godliness requires deliberate training, much like physical discipline.","Contentment with godliness, not wealth accumulation, marks healthy spiritual priorities."],
 "expositor":("tozer","Tozer often warned, echoing 1 Timothy 6, that the danger is not money itself but the love of it -- a subtle idolatry that can quietly reorder a believer's priorities and affections away from genuine contentment in God."),
 "reflect":"Where might the pursuit or love of financial security be quietly competing with contentment in God as your true security?"},

{"day":70,"book":"2 Timothy","ref":"2 Timothy 1-4","title":"Paul's Final Charge",
 "chapters":[
   ("2 Tim 1","Writing from prison near the end of his life, Paul urges Timothy not to be timid but to 'fan into flame' the gift God gave him, since 'God did not give us a spirit of fear.'"),
   ("2 Tim 2","Paul calls Timothy to endure hardship like a soldier, compete like an athlete, and work like a farmer, and to entrust sound teaching to other 'reliable people' who will teach others also."),
   ("2 Tim 3","Paul warns of increasing difficulty in the last days and reminds Timothy that 'all Scripture is God-breathed and is useful' for teaching, correction, and equipping."),
   ("2 Tim 4","In his final recorded words, Paul declares, 'I have fought the good fight... I have kept the faith,' charging Timothy to 'preach the word' faithfully whether convenient or not."),
 ],
 "themes":["Faithful ministry must be intentionally passed on to the next generation.","Scripture's God-breathed authority equips believers thoroughly for every good work."],
 "expositor":("begg","Begg has often highlighted Paul's charge to Timothy to entrust truth to 'reliable people who will also be qualified to teach others' as a biblical blueprint for discipleship across generations, not merely one-directional mentorship."),
 "reflect":"Who is someone you could intentionally begin entrusting spiritual truth to, the way Paul entrusted it to Timothy?"},

{"day":71,"book":"Titus & Philemon","ref":"Titus 1-3; Philemon","title":"Sound Doctrine, Good Works, and a Changed Relationship",
 "chapters":[
   ("Titus 1","Paul instructs Titus to appoint qualified elders across Crete's churches and to silence false teachers characterized by empty, disruptive talk."),
   ("Titus 2","Paul outlines godly conduct for different groups within the church, grounding it all in the grace of God that teaches believers to say no to ungodliness."),
   ("Titus 3","Paul reminds Titus that believers were saved 'not because of righteous things we had done, but because of his mercy,' and should devote themselves to good works as a result, not a cause, of salvation."),
   ("Philemon","Paul appeals to Philemon to receive back his runaway slave Onesimus, now a believer, 'no longer as a slave, but... as a dear brother' -- a personal case study in gospel-transformed relationships."),
 ],
 "themes":["Grace, not human effort, is both the ground and the teacher of godly living.","The gospel reshapes even deeply unequal social relationships from the inside out."],
 "expositor":("bruce","F. F. Bruce observed that Philemon shows the gospel working like leaven within an unjust social structure rather than through immediate revolution -- planting a seed ('no longer as a slave... but a brother') that would, over time, help undermine slavery's entire moral foundation."),
 "reflect":"Is there a relationship where the gospel calls you to a change of status or posture -- from resentment to welcome, from distance to genuine kinship?"},

{"day":72,"book":"Hebrews","ref":"Hebrews 1-3","title":"Christ Superior to Angels and Moses",
 "chapters":[
   ("Heb 1","The author opens by declaring the Son as the final, superior revelation of God -- 'the exact representation of his being' -- far greater than even the highest angels."),
   ("Heb 2","The author warns against drifting away from 'so great a salvation,' explaining that Christ became fully human, sharing in flesh and blood, so He could die and free those held captive by fear of death."),
   ("Heb 3","The author compares Jesus favorably to Moses -- faithful as a son over God's house, rather than merely a faithful servant within it -- and warns against Israel's wilderness pattern of unbelief and hardened hearts."),
 ],
 "themes":["Jesus is God's final, superior revelation, surpassing even the greatest figures of the old covenant.","Persistent, hardened unbelief -- not merely occasional doubt -- is the real spiritual danger warned against."],
 "expositor":("bruce","F. F. Bruce, whose landmark commentary remains a standard reference on Hebrews, emphasized that the book's repeated 'how much more' comparisons (angels, Moses, priests) are building one sustained argument: since the old covenant mediators were merely shadows, turning back from Christ to them would be a tragic downgrade, not a safe retreat."),
 "reflect":"Is there an area where spiritual drift -- not dramatic rebellion, just slow neglect -- has quietly crept into your walk with Christ?"},

{"day":73,"book":"Hebrews","ref":"Hebrews 4-6","title":"A Better Rest, A Great High Priest",
 "chapters":[
   ("Heb 4","The author describes a 'Sabbath-rest' still available to God's people through faith, and presents Jesus as a sympathetic high priest who was tempted in every way, inviting believers to 'approach the throne of grace with confidence.'"),
   ("Heb 5","The author explains Christ's priesthood 'in the order of Melchizedek,' then rebukes the readers for spiritual immaturity, still needing milk when they should be ready for solid teaching."),
   ("Heb 6","The author issues a severe warning against apostasy while expressing confidence in his readers' genuine faith, anchoring hope in God's unchangeable promise and oath."),
 ],
 "themes":["Jesus' sympathy as high priest invites bold, confident approach to God, not fearful distance.","Spiritual maturity requires moving beyond elementary teaching toward deeper understanding and practice."],
 "expositor":("spurgeon","Spurgeon preached often on Hebrews 4:16's invitation to 'approach the throne of grace with confidence,' insisting that believers too easily crawl toward God in timid uncertainty when Scripture actually invites them to come boldly, because Christ Himself has already opened the way."),
 "reflect":"Do you tend to approach God more with timid uncertainty or with the bold confidence Hebrews 4:16 actually invites?"},

{"day":74,"book":"Hebrews","ref":"Hebrews 7-9","title":"A New and Better Covenant",
 "chapters":[
   ("Heb 7","The author develops Melchizedek's mysterious priesthood (from Genesis 14) as a picture of Christ's permanent, superior priesthood, unlike the temporary, repeatedly-dying Levitical priests."),
   ("Heb 8","The author announces a genuinely 'new covenant,' quoting Jeremiah's prophecy, in which God's law is written on hearts rather than merely on stone tablets."),
   ("Heb 9","The author contrasts the old covenant's repeated animal sacrifices and earthly tabernacle with Christ's once-for-all sacrifice, which secures 'eternal redemption' and access to the true, heavenly sanctuary."),
 ],
 "themes":["Christ's priesthood is permanent and effective, unlike the temporary, repetitive old covenant system.","Christ's single sacrifice fully and finally accomplishes what endless animal sacrifices never could."],
 "expositor":("sproul","Sproul emphasized that Hebrews 9's contrast between repeated sacrifices and Christ's 'once for all' offering underscores a core Reformation conviction: nothing needs to be added to Christ's finished work, since His single sacrifice was fully and permanently sufficient."),
 "reflect":"Are there ways you've functionally treated your standing with God as needing repeated 're-earning,' rather than resting in Christ's finished, once-for-all sacrifice?"},

{"day":75,"book":"Hebrews","ref":"Hebrews 10-11","title":"One Sacrifice, A Great Cloud of Witnesses",
 "chapters":[
   ("Heb 10","The author declares Christ's single sacrifice has 'made perfect forever those who are being made holy,' urging believers to draw near to God, hold fast their hope, and not neglect meeting together."),
   ("Heb 11","The 'hall of faith' surveys figures from Abel to the prophets who lived by faith in promises they never fully saw fulfilled in their lifetimes, defining faith as 'confidence in what we hope for and assurance about what we do not see.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["Christ's finished work secures a definitive, once-for-all standing before God for believers.","True faith trusts God's promises even without seeing their complete fulfillment in this life."],
 "expositor":("spurgeon","Spurgeon's sermon 'Faith' on Hebrews 11 described the great cloud of witnesses as a company who 'died in faith, not having received the promises,' proof that genuine faith does not require seeing the end from the beginning to remain steadfast."),
 "reflect":"Hebrews 11 defines faith as confidence in what we cannot yet see. What is one promise of God you are currently holding onto by faith alone?"},

{"day":76,"book":"Hebrews","ref":"Hebrews 12-13","title":"Running the Race, Living Sacrifice",
 "chapters":[
   ("Heb 12","The author calls believers to run their race with endurance, 'fixing our eyes on Jesus,' and interprets hardship as loving fatherly discipline rather than random misfortune."),
   ("Heb 13","The author closes with practical exhortations -- hospitality, care for prisoners, honoring marriage, contentment, and submission to godly leaders -- grounded in the reminder that 'Jesus Christ is the same yesterday and today and forever.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["Hardship, rightly understood, can be a sign of God's fatherly care rather than His absence.","Fixing attention on Jesus sustains endurance through a long, difficult race."],
 "expositor":("wiersbe","Wiersbe noted that Hebrews 12's picture of discipline is drawn from loving Jewish and Greco-Roman family training, not harsh punishment -- meaning God's discipline in a believer's life aims at maturity and holiness, not merely correction for its own sake."),
 "reflect":"Is there a current hardship you might be able to reinterpret as God's fatherly training rather than His abandonment or punishment?"},

{"day":77,"book":"James","ref":"James 1-5","title":"Faith That Works",
 "chapters":[
   ("James 1","James calls trials an occasion for joy since they produce endurance, urges believers to be 'quick to listen, slow to speak,' and insists genuine religion cares practically for orphans and widows."),
   ("James 2","James famously insists that 'faith without deeds is dead,' using Abraham and Rahab as examples, and rebukes favoritism shown toward the wealthy over the poor within the church."),
   ("James 3","James warns of the tongue's outsized power to bless or destroy, comparing it to a small rudder steering a huge ship, and contrasts worldly and heavenly wisdom."),
   ("James 4","James confronts quarrels rooted in selfish desires, calls for humble submission to God rather than worldly friendship, and warns against arrogant, presumptuous planning that ignores God's will."),
   ("James 5","James rebukes wealthy oppressors, calls for patient endurance modeled on the prophets and Job, and emphasizes the power of persistent, faith-filled prayer, especially for the sick and the wandering."),
 ],
 "themes":["Genuine faith always produces visible, practical fruit -- it cannot remain merely intellectual.","Care for the vulnerable, control of speech, and dependence on God in prayer all mark authentic faith."],
 "expositor":("macarthur","MacArthur has taught that James 2's 'faith without deeds is dead' is not adding works to grace as a requirement for salvation, but insisting that genuine, grace-given faith simply cannot help but produce a changed, active life -- the fruit, not the root, of true conversion."),
 "reflect":"Where in your life would an honest observer see the clearest evidence that your faith is more than just words?"},

{"day":78,"book":"1 Peter","ref":"1 Peter 1-5","title":"Living Hope Amid Suffering",
 "chapters":[
   ("1 Pet 1","Peter grounds believers' 'living hope' in Christ's resurrection, describing their trials as refining like gold and calling them to holy living because they've been redeemed by Christ's precious blood."),
   ("1 Pet 2","Peter describes believers as 'living stones' being built into a spiritual house and 'a chosen people,' called to live such good lives among unbelievers that even critics see their good deeds."),
   ("1 Pet 3","Peter instructs on marriage and calls all believers to unity, humility, and readiness to gently explain their hope, even while suffering unjustly for doing good."),
   ("1 Pet 4","Peter urges believers not to be surprised by 'fiery trials,' but to rejoice in sharing Christ's sufferings, and to use their gifts to serve one another faithfully."),
   ("1 Pet 5","Peter instructs elders to shepherd God's flock willingly and humbly, calls believers to resist the devil, 'a roaring lion,' and closes with confidence in God's care for the humble."),
 ],
 "themes":["Suffering for doing good, though painful, can refine faith and display Christ to onlookers.","Humility -- toward God, toward one another, and under trial -- runs through the whole letter."],
 "expositor":("carson","Carson has pointed out that Peter's call to 'always be prepared to give an answer' for one's hope in 1 Peter 3 assumes ordinary Christian living itself provokes genuine questions from watching neighbors -- apologetics rooted in a visibly different life, not merely clever argument."),
 "reflect":"Does your current way of living provoke the kind of curious questions from others that 1 Peter 3:15 assumes it should?"},

{"day":79,"book":"2 Peter","ref":"2 Peter 1-3","title":"Guarding Against False Teachers",
 "chapters":[
   ("2 Pet 1","Peter urges believers to add virtue, knowledge, self-control, and other qualities to their faith to keep from being ineffective, grounding his teaching in his own eyewitness experience of Christ's glory."),
   ("2 Pet 2","Peter issues a sharp warning against false teachers who exploit believers with greed and immorality, comparing their coming judgment to that of fallen angels and the flood generation."),
   ("2 Pet 3","Peter answers scoffers who mock the delay of Christ's return, explaining that God's patience allows more time for repentance, and describes the eventual dissolution and renewal of creation."),
 ],
 "themes":["Growing Christian character is an active, deliberate pursuit, not a passive byproduct.","God's apparent slowness in fulfilling promises is patience, not indifference or failure."],
 "expositor":("ryrie","Ryrie noted that 2 Peter 1's call to actively 'add' virtues to faith counters any notion that spiritual growth happens automatically -- genuine faith initiates a lifelong, deliberate pursuit of Christlike character."),
 "reflect":"Which quality from Peter's list (faith, virtue, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, kindness, love) do you most need to deliberately pursue right now?"},

{"day":80,"book":"1 John","ref":"1 John 1-5","title":"Walking in Light, Love, and Assurance",
 "chapters":[
   ("1 John 1","John insists that walking 'in the light' means honest confession of sin, promising that God is faithful to forgive and purify those who confess rather than deny their sin."),
   ("1 John 2","John describes Christ as our 'advocate' when we sin, warns against loving 'the world,' and urges believers to test claims against the truth already taught to them."),
   ("1 John 3","John marvels at the Father's love that makes believers His children, calling for love expressed in action and truth, not mere words."),
   ("1 John 4","John gives the test for true teaching (confessing Christ came in the flesh) and the famous declaration that 'God is love,' insisting that genuine love for God necessarily produces love for others."),
   ("1 John 5","John explains that belief in Jesus as the Son of God brings genuine assurance of eternal life, closing with confidence that believers can know -- not merely hope -- that they possess it."),
 ],
 "themes":["Honest confession, not denial or pretense, is the pathway to ongoing fellowship with God.","Genuine love for God is inseparable from genuine, active love for other people."],
 "expositor":("stott","Stott's commentary on 1 John highlighted its three repeated 'tests' of genuine faith -- believing rightly about Christ, obeying His commands, and loving other believers -- as together giving assurance without either legalistic anxiety or false presumption."),
 "reflect":"Of John's three tests (right belief, obedience, love for others), which would you say is currently the weakest evidence in your own life?"},

{"day":81,"book":"2 John, 3 John & Jude","ref":"2 John; 3 John; Jude","title":"Truth, Hospitality, and Contending for the Faith",
 "chapters":[
   ("2 John","John commends walking in truth and love, but warns against extending hospitality that would support traveling false teachers who deny Christ came in the flesh."),
   ("3 John","John commends Gaius for his faithful hospitality toward traveling missionaries, and rebukes Diotrephes for his prideful, controlling refusal to welcome them."),
   ("Jude","Jude urgently calls believers to 'contend for the faith' against false teachers who had quietly infiltrated the church, twisting grace into an excuse for immorality, and closes with a soaring doxology of God's power to keep believers from stumbling."),
 ],
 "themes":["Discernment about whom to support matters as much as general hospitality.","Grace properly understood produces holiness, never an excuse for immorality."],
 "expositor":("baucham","Baucham has often emphasized Jude's call to 'contend for the faith' as a permanent, non-optional charge for every generation of the church, since false teaching that twists grace into license for sin does not stop reappearing in new forms over time."),
 "reflect":"Is there a teaching or influence in your own life you've extended uncritical support to, without carefully weighing whether it truly honors Christ?"},
]
